\subsection*{Recurring Notation}

We list notation that is commonly used throughout the paper below. More specialized notation is specified in the corresponding section.
Throughout, a \emph{constant} is a number independent of $n$, $k$, and $d$. It may depend on fixed local dimensions $q$ and $p$, uniform upper bounds on stabilizer weights, qudit degrees, vertex-set sizes, graph degrees, cycle weights and edge incidences, and decoration-path lengths, as well as a uniform positive lower bound on the relevant expansion. These bounds are held fixed as the code family grows.

\begin{longtable}{p{0.25\linewidth}p{0.71\linewidth}}
    \toprule
    Notation & Denotes \\
        \midrule
    \endhead
        $\cC $ & Original QLDPC Pauli stabilizer code over qudits with dimension $q$, with $n$ physical qudits, $k$ logical qudits, and distance $d$. The $n$ qudits are referred to as matter qudits.\\
        $\cC'$ & Gauged code obtained by deforming $\cC$.
        \\
        $\T = \bigotimes_{v\in \vertexV}\T_v, \quad \T^p = \id$ & Transversal logical Clifford operator $\T$. $\T_v$ is its constant-sized, local operators and $p$ is the order.
        \\
        $p$ & Order of $\T$ and the dimension of the gauge qudits.
        \\
        $\mathcal{M}$ & Set of matter qudits in the original code.
        \\
        $\graphG = (\vertexV, \edgeE, \faceF)$ & Gauging $2$-complex or gauging graph. Vertices correspond to local operators $\T_v$. Edges correspond to ancilla or gauge qudits. Faces correspond with cycle stabilizers.
        \\
        $\Sigma : \mathcal{M} \to \vertexV$ & Assignment of matter qudits in the original code to vertex sets. $\Sigma^{-1}(v)$ is the constant-sized set of qudits associated with $v\in \vertexV$, acted on by $\T_v$.
        \\
        $\Xp^a, \Zp^\chi$ & Generalized Pauli shift and clock operators for the Abelian symmetry $\Agrp$, with $a\in\Agrp$, $\chi\in\widehat{\Agrp}$, and $\Zp^\chi\Xp^a=\omega^\chi(a)\Xp^a\Zp^\chi$. The gauge qudits have dimension $|\Agrp|$.
        \\
        $X,Z$ & Generalized Pauli shift and clock operators on $q$-dimensional matter qudits where $ZX = XZ e^{2\pi i /q}$
        \\

        $\ComGrp{A}{B}$ & Group commutator $ABA^\dagger B^\dagger$. 
        \\

        $\av$ & Vertex stabilizer. The product of all vertex stabilizers is the transversal Clifford operator $\prod_{v\in \vertexV}\av = \T$.
        \\
        $\bp$ & Cycle stabilizers which are $\mathsf{Z}$-type Pauli operators with support on cycles $ f\in \faceF$.
        \\
        $S_i, \bar{S}_i$  & Stabilizer of $\cC$ and symmetrized stabilizer $\bar{S} = \frac{1}{p}\sum_{a = 0}^{p-1} \T^a S (\T^a)^\dagger$.
        \\

        $\mathbb{S}_i = \mathfrak{C}_2(S_i) \mathrm{\ or\ }\mathfrak{C}(S_i)$ & Decorated counterpart in the gauged code $\cC'$ of a stabilizer $S_i$ of the code $\cC$. $\mathfrak{C}_2$ denotes the decoration map when $p=2$. If $p>2$, the decoration map is $\mathfrak{C}$.\\ 


        $d_\mathrm{g},d_\mathrm{f}$ & $d_\mathrm{g}$ is the distance of $\cC'$ to data qudit Pauli errors. $d_\mathrm{f}$ denotes the spacetime fault distance of the gauging protocol (Sec.~\ref{sec:spacetime-distance}).\\
        \bottomrule
    \\
    \caption{Essential notation used throughout Sections~\ref{sec:gauging-procedure},~\ref{sec:space-distance},~\ref{sec:spacetime-distance}.}
    \label{tab:notation-lite}\\
\end{longtable}

